                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Juneau AK",
                "headline": "Hydrologic Outlook issued October 12 at 10:07AM AKDT by NWS Juneau AK",
                "description": "ESFAJK\n\nAn atmospheric river will move into the region Thursday, driving\nheavy rain across much of the Panhandle into the weekend. However,\nareas south of Frederick Sound will likely see the heaviest rain.\nAnticipate 24 hour rainfall amounts to reach 3 to 5 inches at sea\nlevel for many communities south of Frederick Sound, with localized\nareas near Ketchikan and Misty Fjords closer to 4 to 6 inches,\nespecially in the mountains. At this time, the primary threat is many\nrivers and streams reaching bankfull conditions with ponding of water\non roads and areas of poor drainage. Mariners, check those bilge\npumps.\n\nSignificant rises and flooding in rivers is possible but at this time\nthere exists large variability in how rain develops and persists\nacross the southern Panhandle.\n\nStay tuned to weather.gov/juneau as we continue to assess the\nsituation and make updates to the forecast.",
                "instruction": null,
                "response": "Monitor",
